  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 115 AM CDT Tue Oct 20 2026\n\nTODAY THROUGH TUESDAY... \nBeautiful sunny and mild weather will continue through \nSaturday. There are no major changes to the forecast, as we\ncontinued to lean on the high side of the forecast guidance for\ntemperatures. Afternoon highs will continue to be 15 to 20 \ndegrees above normal, reaching the 70s across most locations \nsouth of I-94, with upper 60s north of I-94. \n\nThe driving force behind this prolonged stretch of warm weather \nis a large Upper Level ridge across the Mississippi River \nValley, with low level southerly flow beneath it. On Saturday, a\nmid latitude cyclone will develop across central Canada and \ntrack toward Hudson Bay. This system will sweep a cold front \nthrough the region, and bring scattered rain showers. Rainfall \namounts should be light given the lack of forcing, with most \nlocations seeing less than a quarter inch. \n\nTemperatures next week will be much cooler following the passage\nof this aforementioned cold front, but since we're starting out\nso warm, the end result will be temperatures closer to the\nseasonal average for late October. What that does mean is that\nSaturday could be the last 70 degree day of the year. In the\nTwin Cities, there have only been 25 years in the climate record\ndating back to 1873 where the last 70 degree day occurred in \nNovember.",
